## 5. Setting Up Hybrid Python/C++ Registration

Registration order matters — importing in the wrong order leads to an error. **First load the C++ library holding the operator definition, then call the `torch.library` registration APIs.** Three ways:

### Method 1 — Dummy Python module via `PYBIND11_MODULE` (breaks `Py_LIMITED_API`)

```cpp
// in, say, not_agnostic/csrc/extension_BAD.cpp
#include <pybind11/pybind11.h>

PYBIND11_MODULE("_C", m) {}
```
```python
# in, say, extension/__init__.py
from . import _C
```
> This does **not** compile with `Py_LIMITED_API` because pybind11 doesn't promise to use only the stable limited CPython API. You cannot build a CPython agnostic wheel this way.

### Method 2 — Stable API dummy module (used in this tutorial)

Compiles with `-DPy_LIMITED_API=0x03090000` and creates a dummy `_C` module. See [extension_cpp/__init__.py](https://github.com/pytorch/extension-cpp/blob/38ec45e/extension_cpp/__init__.py) and [extension_cpp/csrc/muladd.cpp](https://github.com/pytorch/extension-cpp/blob/38ec45e/extension_cpp/csrc/muladd.cpp).

```cpp
#include <Python.h>

extern "C" {
  /* Creates a dummy empty _C module that can be imported from Python.
    The import from Python will load the .so consisting of this file
    in this extension, so that the TORCH_LIBRARY static initializers
    below are run. */
  PyObject* PyInit__C(void)
  {
      static struct PyModuleDef module_def = {
          PyModuleDef_HEAD_INIT,
          "_C",   /* name of module */
          NULL,   /* module documentation, may be NULL */
          -1,     /* size of per-interpreter state of the module,
                    or -1 if the module keeps state in global variables. */
          NULL,   /* methods */
      };
      return PyModule_Create(&module_def);
  }
}
```
```python
from . import _C
```

### Method 3 — `torch.ops.load_library` (avoids `Python.h`)

No `_C` module is created, so `import _C` is impossible. Use `torch.ops.load_library` to load the `.so` files:

```python
import torch
from pathlib import Path

so_files = list(Path(__file__).parent.glob("_C*.so"))
assert (
    len(so_files) == 1
), f"Expected one _C*.so file, found {len(so_files)}"
torch.ops.load_library(so_files[0])

from . import ops
```

